 "questions": [
  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L12_001_Counting Steps",
   "display_name": "Counting Steps",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Welcome to Lab 12! Two programs can give the same answer, yet one may take a second and the other a day. To compare <b>algorithms</b> (step-by-step methods), we <b>count the basic operations</b> they perform, and ask how that count <b>grows</b> when the input gets bigger.</p>\n<p>Look at these two loops for a list of <code>n</code> items:</p>\n<pre>n = 5\none_pass = 0\nfor i in range(n):\n    one_pass += 1\nall_pairs = 0\nfor i in range(n):\n    for j in range(i + 1, n):\n        all_pairs += 1\nprint(one_pass, all_pairs)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>5 10</pre>\n<p>The first does <code>n</code> steps: we say it takes <b>O(n)</b> time (\"order n\", or <b>linear</b>): double <code>n</code>, double the work. The second visits every pair <code>i &lt; j</code>, which is <code>n * (n - 1) / 2</code> steps: <b>O(n²)</b> (<b>quadratic</b>): double <code>n</code>, about four times the work. The <b>O notation</b> keeps only how fast the count grows, ignoring constant factors like <code>/ 2</code>:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"5\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;margin:6px 0 12px;font-size:14px;text-align:right\"><tr><th>n (items)</th><th>O(log n): halving</th><th>O(n): one pass</th><th>O(n&sup2;): all pairs</th></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>4</td><td>10</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>7</td><td>100</td><td>10,000</td></tr><tr><td>1,000</td><td>10</td><td>1,000</td><td>1,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>20</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>1,000,000,000,000</td></tr></table>\n<p>(<code>x += 1</code> is short for <code>x = x + 1</code>.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>count_triples(n)</code>, which <b>counts with loops</b> (not with a formula) how many triples of indexes <code>i &lt; j &lt; k</code> there are with all three from <code>0</code> to <code>n - 1</code>, and returns that count. This grows as <b>O(n³)</b>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>count_triples(4)</code> returns <code>4</code> (the triples are 012, 013, 023, 123).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def count_triples(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(count_triples(4))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_triples(4))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "4",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_triples(3), count_triples(2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1 0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(count_triples(10))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "120",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L12_002_Counting Halvings",
   "display_name": "Counting Halvings",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Some algorithms throw away <b>half</b> of the remaining work at every step. Starting from <code>n</code>, how many halvings until only 1 is left? For <code>n = 16</code>: 16, 8, 4, 2, 1, that is 4 halvings. This number is the <b>logarithm</b> of <code>n</code> in base 2, written <code>log n</code>. It grows <b>very slowly</b>: <code>log</code> of a million is only about 20. Algorithms whose step count grows like this take <b>O(log n)</b> time (<b>logarithmic</b>).</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>halvings(n)</code>, which returns how many times a positive integer <code>n</code> (int &gt; 0) can be replaced by <code>n // 2</code> before it becomes 1 (for <code>n = 1</code> the answer is 0). Use a <code>while</code> loop.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>halvings(16)</code> returns <code>4</code> and <code>halvings(20)</code> returns <code>4</code> (20, 10, 5, 2, 1).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def halvings(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(halvings(16))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(halvings(16))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "4",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(halvings(20), halvings(1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "4 0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(halvings(1000000))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "19",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "L12_003_Linear Search",
   "display_name": "Linear Search",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The simplest way to find a value in a list is <b>linear search</b>: look at the items one by one from the start, until you find it (or reach the end).</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">L =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">7</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(42,112,170);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">9</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(63,146,206);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">6</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#16202b;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#16202b;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#16202b;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#16202b;white-space:nowrap\">&uarr;<br>found</td><td style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#16202b;white-space:nowrap\"></td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">searching for 4: look at 7, 3, 9, 4, found at index 4 - 1 = 3 after 4 looks</div>\n<p><b>Pseudocode</b> (a plain description of the steps):</p>\n<pre>linear_search(L, x):\n    for i = 0, 1, ..., len(L) - 1:\n        if probe(L, i) == x:\n            return i\n    return -1</pre>\n<p><b>How we count:</b> the code box starts with a helper function <code>probe(L, i)</code>, which simply returns <code>L[i]</code>. Use it <b>every time</b> you look at an item (instead of writing <code>L[i]</code> yourself). The tests count your calls to <code>probe</code>: this is the number of items your search looked at. Do not change the helper functions.</p>\n<p>In the worst case (the value is not there, or is last), linear search looks at all <code>n</code> items: it is <b>O(n)</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>linear_search(L, x)</code> following the pseudocode exactly: return the index of the first <code>x</code> in the list <code>L</code>, or <code>-1</code> if it is not there.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>linear_search([7, 3, 9, 4, 6], 4)</code> returns <code>3</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# ---- Helper functions: do not change them; the tests count how often you call them ----\ndef probe(L, i):\n    # Look at the item at index i. Use this EVERY time you read an item.\n    return L[i]\n\n\ndef linear_search(L, x):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(linear_search([7, 3, 9, 4, 6], 4))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "_c = {\"probe\": 0}\n_probe = probe\ndef probe(*args):\n    _c[\"probe\"] += 1\n    return _probe(*args)\nprint(linear_search([7, 3, 9, 4, 6], 4), 'looks:', _c['probe'])",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "3 looks: 4",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "_c = {\"probe\": 0}\n_probe = probe\ndef probe(*args):\n    _c[\"probe\"] += 1\n    return _probe(*args)\nprint(linear_search([7, 3, 9, 4, 6], 5), 'looks:', _c['probe'])",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "-1 looks: 5",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "_c = {\"probe\": 0}\n_probe = probe\ndef probe(*args):\n    _c[\"probe\"] += 1\n    return _probe(*args)\nprint(linear_search([], 1), 'looks:', _c['probe'])",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "-1 looks: 0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "_c = {\"probe\": 0}\n_probe = probe\ndef probe(*args):\n    _c[\"probe\"] += 1\n    return _probe(*args)\nL = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(linear_search(L, 8), 'looks:', _c['probe'])",
     "stdin": "8 1 8 2",
