  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L12_004_Search From The End",
   "display_name": "Search From The End",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Linear search can also run <b>backwards</b>, to find the <b>last</b> occurrence of a value. The number of looks then depends on how far from the end the value is.</p>\n<pre>last_index(L, x):\n    for i = len(L) - 1, len(L) - 2, ..., 0:\n        if probe(L, i) == x:\n            return i\n    return -1</pre>\n<p>(<code>range(len(L) - 1, -1, -1)</code> gives the indexes from the last down to 0.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>last_index(L, x)</code> following the pseudocode exactly, using <code>probe</code> for every look.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>last_index([4, 1, 4, 2], 4)</code> returns <code>2</code> after 2 looks.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# ---- Helper functions: do not change them; the tests count how often you call them ----\ndef probe(L, i):\n    # Look at the item at index i. Use this EVERY time you read an item.\n    return L[i]\n\n\ndef last_index(L, x):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(last_index([4, 1, 4, 2], 4))\n",
