  {
   "xml_index": 11,
   "xml_name": "L12_011_Counting Inversions",
   "display_name": "Counting Inversions",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>An <b>inversion</b> is a pair of positions <code>i &lt; j</code> whose items are in the wrong order: <code>L[i] &gt; L[j]</code>. A sorted list has 0 inversions; a reversed list of <code>n</code> different items has <code>n * (n - 1) / 2</code>.</p>\n<p>Each swap of two <b>neighbours</b> in the wrong order removes exactly one inversion. That is why bubble sort and insertion sort always make exactly as many swaps as the list has inversions (check: <code>[5, 1, 4, 2, 8]</code> has 4 inversions, and both made 4 swaps).</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>inversions(L)</code>, which returns the number of inversions in the list <code>L</code> (use two nested loops over the pairs; no helpers needed).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>inversions([5, 1, 4, 2, 8])</code> returns <code>4</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def inversions(L):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(inversions([5, 1, 4, 2, 8]))\n",
