  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L12_012_Merging Sorted Lists",
   "display_name": "Merging Sorted Lists",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Faster sorting methods split the work. A key step is <b>merging</b>: two lists that are <b>already sorted</b> can be combined into one sorted list in a single pass. Keep a position in each list; repeatedly take the smaller of the two current items; when one list runs out, take the rest of the other.</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">a =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">9</td></tr></table><table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">b =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(45,118,176);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(60,140,200);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">10</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">12</td></tr></table><table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">merged:</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(38,106,165);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(45,118,176);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(60,140,200);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">9</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(67,152,211);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">10</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">12</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">each step takes the smaller front item: O(len(a) + len(b))</div>\n<pre>merge(a, b):\n    result = empty list, i = 0, j = 0\n    while i &lt; len(a) and j &lt; len(b):\n        if a[i] &lt;= b[j]: append a[i] to result; i = i + 1\n        else:            append b[j] to result; j = j + 1\n    append the rest of a (from i) and the rest of b (from j)</pre>\n<p>This is <b>O(n)</b>. (<b>Merge sort</b> splits a list in halves, sorts each half the same way, and merges them: <b>O(n log n)</b>.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>merge(a, b)</code>, which returns a new sorted list with all the items of the sorted lists <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>. Do not use <code>sort</code> or <code>sorted</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>merge([1, 4, 9], [2, 3, 10, 12])</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 10, 12]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def merge(a, b):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(merge([1, 4, 9], [2, 3, 10, 12]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
