  {
   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "L12_015_Comparisons As A Formula",
   "display_name": "Comparisons As A Formula",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Counting by hand connects code to O notation. Now the other way round: predict the count <b>without running the algorithm</b>.</p>\n<ul><li>Bubble sort (without early stop) and selection sort always make <code>n * (n - 1) / 2</code> comparisons.</li><li>Binary search on <code>n</code> items (the first pseudocode) looks at most <code>halvings(n) + 1</code> times, where <code>halvings</code> is your function from question 2.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write two functions for a non-negative integer <code>n</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>sort_comparisons(n)</code> returns <code>n * (n - 1) // 2</code>;</li><li><code>binary_worst(n)</code> returns the maximum number of looks of binary search on <code>n</code> items: <code>0</code> for <code>n = 0</code>, otherwise the number of times <code>n</code> can be halved (with <code>// 2</code>) before reaching 1, plus 1.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>sort_comparisons(1000)</code> returns <code>499500</code> while <code>binary_worst(1000)</code> returns <code>10</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def sort_comparisons(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef binary_worst(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(sort_comparisons(1000), binary_worst(1000))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(sort_comparisons(1000), binary_worst(1000))",
