  {
   "xml_index": 16,
   "xml_name": "L12_016_Choose The Right Search",
   "display_name": "Choose The Right Search",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Many searches in the <b>same</b> big sorted list: linear search would need about <code>n</code> looks each; binary search only about <code>log n</code>. The hidden tests of this question use a list of 100000 items and 50 searches, and allow at most <b>1000 looks in total</b>, so only an O(log n) method will pass.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>count_present(L, targets)</code>, where <code>L</code> is a list sorted from small to large and <code>targets</code> is a list of values. Return how many of the values in <code>targets</code> are in <code>L</code>. Read items of <code>L</code> only through <code>probe(L, i)</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>count_present([1, 3, 5, 7], [3, 4, 7])</code> returns <code>2</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# ---- Helper functions: do not change them; the tests count how often you call them ----\ndef probe(L, i):\n    # Look at the item at index i. Use this EVERY time you read an item.\n    return L[i]\n\n\ndef count_present(L, targets):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(count_present([1, 3, 5, 7], [3, 4, 7]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "_c = {\"probe\": 0}\n_probe = probe\ndef probe(*args):\n    _c[\"probe\"] += 1\n    return _probe(*args)\nprint(count_present([1, 3, 5, 7], [3, 4, 7]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "2",
     "extra": "",
